    "organization_prompt": "# Task\n\nUse the `tools.profile` namespace to generate and save a separate Profile for every agent identity.\n\n## 0. Data sources and interfaces\n\nUse `tools.inspect` to discover the members of the `tools.profile` namespace and its internal method and field declarations.\nRead `annotation`, `tools`, and `profiles` before proceeding; do not assume field names from memory.\n\n## 1. Profile selection\n\n1. **Identity enumeration**: Create one Profile per agent identity. Determine identities in this order:\n   - An explicit identity list in the task context;\n   - Existing identities in `profiles`;\n   - If neither exists, infer identities by clustering the capabilities in `tools` (such as Coding, Research, and Web), and explain the inference in the final report.\n2. **Required Profiles**:\n   - `通用`: Include every command in `tools` so all capabilities remain discoverable.\n   - `基础`: Include only `bash` and `edit`, both at level 2.\n3. **Identity Profiles**: Include only commands relevant to that identity and minimize functional overlap between identities.\n4. Reuse: Commands may be reused across Profiles, but each command may be mounted only once within a Profile.\n5. Naming: Use Chinese for Profile names and `brief`, choosing natural names by purpose rather than literal translations. Every Profile must have a short Chinese `brief`.\n\n## 2. Command descriptions\n\n- Write every command description in English, summarizing its core function in one sentence.\n- Do not describe parameters, options, return values, or examples.\n- Remove redundant wording while retaining enough meaning to distinguish similar commands.\n- Apply these rules to every Profile, including level 2 commands. Pin level does not affect description length.\n\n## 3. Pin levels\n\nDecide based on `totalCalls`, whether user interaction is required, and the Profile's purpose:\n\n- **Level 2**: Extremely frequent commands and user interaction commands. Pin the full command definition.\n- **Level 1**: Common commands. Pin only the name and a short English description.\n- **Unpinned**: Discover other commands through namespaces on demand.\n\nMinimize pinned items to reduce context usage. The same command may have different levels in different Profiles.\n\n## 4. Namespace structure\n\n- Group by functional meaning, allow multiple nesting levels, and use short, clear names.\n- Within each Profile, assign each command to exactly one namespace.\n- Optimize namespace structure independently for each Profile.\n- Avoid intermediate layers with only one member and meaningless nesting.\n\n## 5. Saving workflow\n\n1. Construct complete JSON conforming to `annotation` for each Profile.\n2. If a Profile with the same name already exists, inspect it with `load` first. Use `save` for a complete rebuild and `apply` for local changes.\n3. Call `tools.profile.save` separately for each Profile; do not combine them into one call.\n4. Do not call `remove`, `switch`, or `setdefault` unless the task explicitly requires it.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\nAfter saving, check each requirement:\n\n- The command set in `通用` equals the complete set in `tools`.\n- `基础` contains only `bash` and `edit`.\n- No command is mounted more than once within a Profile.\n- Every description is a short English sentence.\n- Every Profile has a Chinese `brief`.\n- Every identity has a corresponding Profile.\n\n## 7. Report\n\nBriefly list each Profile's name, purpose, and command counts at level 2, level 1, and unpinned. Report inferred identities or unclassified commands separately.",
